Subject: [#94-12-42] The Machinery of Friedman

 fhapgood@world.std.com  (Fred Hapgood) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-12-8:
>Let's say I want to live in a society that prohibits child abuse
>and am willing to pay for my preferences.  How do I do that under
>Friedman's scheme?  I assume that me and everybody who feels the

 You reward any legal system that prevents its customers from engaging
in child abuse. For example, you might set up a charitable orginization
which channels the contributions of everyone who shares your belief to
the appropriate legal system companies. Those companies would then be able
to charge less than their competitors, which would mean that anyone wanting
the right to abuse children would have to pay for it (or do without legal
protection). If child abuse is sufficiently unpopular, maybe so few people
would be willing to subscribe to a legal system that permitted it that no
company would provide such a legal system.
 There are some nontrivial free-rider problems here, but that is true of
monopolistic legal systems also.

>This raises a theoretical point, which is that Friedman
>everywhere in this book defines government as 'a system of legal
>coercion', and seems to want his book to be about building
>alternatives to such systems.  But the defining feature of the
>justice system, civil or criminal, is that it can compel
>conformance with its judgements. This is what distinguishes it

 The "coercion" used by Friedman's legal systems amounts to saying, as a
last resort, that thieves or murderers can do anything they want to the
lawbreaker. This will probably have many similar effects to jail or execution,
but it has the important difference that it doesn't prevent competition
between legal systems.

>Finally, I don't think for an instant that a system like this
>would lead to a plurality of legal codes.  The law is a social

 I expect less diversity in legal systems than there is in operating systems.
I think the credit card industry is a better example of the diversity that
I would expect. I expect that there will be differences in abortion laws,
and a handfull of other issues that are much less significant than abortion.
