From: fhapgood@world.std.com (Fred Hapgood)
Date: Thu, 1 Dec 1994 16:48:20 -0500
Subject: [#94-12-46] The Machinery of Friedman

Mark Grant read:

> If my PIs are empowered to
> grab your kids (because you are selling their bodies to support
> your drug habit, or refusing to provide them with adequate health
> care, or letting them run around the streets without educating
> them) then they are empowered to act against your interests and
> without your consent.

And replied:

   How are they "empowered" ? Surely you're effectively just
   hiring a bunch of thugs to go around beating up people who
   don't agree with you?

The point here is that any society, including Friedman's, has its
coercions.

One target of these coercions are those who some neutral party
decides are trying to weasel out of a contract. Typically the
targets themselves don't agree with this view; they have their
claims, their counter-assertions. Nonetheless, any society has to
have mechanisms to decide when a person is trying to get out of
meeting an appropriate obligation and then coerce them into
behaving appropriately or compelling them to furnish redress.
Friedman's ideal society certainly does: he touches passingly on
this in an anecdote about a dispute over a TV set.  The story
ends with the TV set being removed from the possession of one of
the actors against his consent.  (p. 116) According to Friedman,
Icelandic society, which he holds up as a model for his ideas,
enforced its judgements by threatening to kill anyone who ignored
them (p. 203).  If government is legal coercion, then these are
governments, at least by my standard of coercion.  

Another target of coercion is people engaging in obnoxious
behavior, even if it's behavior they haven't contracted to
abjure.  Friedman seems to think this won't be a problem, because
people won't be willing to pay to restrain the behavior of
others.  We must live on different planets.  Where I live
a agency promising to launch a war on child abuse would raise
just about as much money as it liked.  (The marketing campaign
writes itself:  Here's a picture of little Jenny, raped and
beaten and buried by her father.  Last year your society was able
to rescue 345 children from a fate like this.  Unfortunately we
were too late to save Jenny. Won't you help us get there on time
for the next little girl?)

An appeal like that would raise billions, and that money would go
quite far, because lots of people would be willing to volunteer
to work for organizations like that.  And of course child abuse
is not the only such cause that could raise funds.  I'm sure you
can think of others.

My point is not that this is a good or bad thing, but that it is
absurd to associate private courts with the elimination of
coercion.  People would go to prison; people would be executed.
(The cost of prison being what it is, probably lots of people
would be executed.)  That's coercion.

Granted, the courts wouldn't be supported by taxes, and so that
element of coercion would be removed, but none of the other
sanctions we bring against behavior could possibly be dispensed
with.  Not if people are going to be held to their word.  And if
you think for a minute that people will tolerate a system in
which they have to sit with their kids and listen while the kid
next door is beaten to death, then, like I said before, we live
on different planets.

